import { assertAuditOpen } from './lane-exports-rpc-ai-auth-completion-guard.mjs';
assertAuditOpen();
// Direct low-level loop and stateful Agent specification. No delegation or source execution.
import {add,save,quote,S,R,test,none} from './author-agent-common.mjs';
const spec={area:'agent',title:'Agent loop, SDK, execution environment and runtime-neutral helpers',introduction:'The active low-level Agent/agentLoop implementation is distinct from the exported AgentHarness v2 scaffold. Entries identify exact call sites and do not infer implementation from comments or type declarations. Existing tests were read, not run. All filesystem/network/process behavior below is a specification of source, not work performed by this audit.',entries:[]};
const tr=(a,b)=>R('ice-agent/src/types.rs',a,b);
const lr=(a,b)=>R('ice-agent/src/agent_loop.rs',a,b);
const ar=(a,b)=>R('ice-agent/src/agent.rs',a,b);
const hr=(a,b)=>R('ice-agent/src/hooks.rs',a,b);
add(spec,1,'Open message union and complete agent event payloads','different-by-design',
 'Expose user, assistant, tool-result and application-defined messages through one typed lifecycle event stream.',
 'AgentMessage is the provider Message union plus every value of the declaration-mergeable CustomAgentMessages interface. No central runtime registration or schema validates a new custom role. AgentContext holds a system-prompt string, message array and optional tools. AgentEvent has the ten variants quoted below. message_start/end and turn_end accept any AgentMessage; message_update also names AgentMessage at the type boundary even though the loop emits assistant updates. Tool result content is text/image only; details is an application-defined required value, usage and addedToolNames are optional, and terminate is not a message role or persistent event. A consumer must not treat arbitrary custom data or tool output as policy. There is no event retention, serial number, size cap or persistence in these types; those belong to higher layers.',
 quote('types',294,319)+'\n'+quote('types',355,437)+'\n'+none,
 S('types',294,437)+'; exported AgentMessage, CustomAgentMessages, AgentContext, AgentEvent, AgentTool/AgentToolResult. Tests: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','should emit events with AgentMessage types')+'; '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','custom message types via convertToLlm')+'.',
 tr(37,54)+'; '+tr(162,220)+'; '+R('ice-agent/src/message.rs',1,160)+'; '+R('ice-agent/src/events.rs',18,110)+'. Rust models custom roles using an open JSON-backed enum instead of TypeScript declaration merging. AgentEvent is not serde-serialized, its update payload is Arc<AssistantMessage> rather than any AgentMessage, and it adds RetryScheduled. Rust details is JSON only, added names is an always-present vector and terminate defaults false. These ownership/type differences are deliberate; any JSONL adapter still needs the exact TypeScript field/tag shapes, not Rust debug output.',[], 'P1',[130,230]);
add(spec,2,'Asynchronous stream-function construction','partial',
 'Allow an embedding to obtain credentials or initialize a provider asynchronously before returning the assistant stream.',
 'StreamFn may return an AssistantMessageEventStream immediately or a Promise of it. streamAssistantResponse awaits the callable before consuming events and then awaits the stream result on completion. There is no construction deadline or cancellation race outside the supplied signal. A rejected construction promise propagates from the raw run function; the Agent wrapper synthesizes a terminal error lifecycle. The public contract asks implementations to put expected request failures into stream error events, but the wrapper also handles violations. Do not replace an async factory with a detached producer silently: factory error timing and cancellation ownership differ.',
 quote('types',20,33)+'\n'+quote('loop',301,308)+'\n'+none,
 S('types',20,33)+'; '+S('loop',281,308)+'; '+S('agent',506,547)+'. Symbols StreamFn/streamAssistantResponse. Tests: '+test('agent.test.ts','full lifecycle events for thrown run failures')+'.',
 R('ice-agent/src/stream_fn.rs',21,41)+'. Rust StreamFn returns a consumer synchronously. The comment at lines4-8 incorrectly says the TypeScript loop never awaits the callable. Rust tests bridge async provider creation with tokio::spawn instead, '+ar(598,617)+'. Add an async factory contract or an explicit adapter whose error/cancellation semantics are documented.',[1,24],'P1',[45,85]);
add(spec,3,'Replaceable default stream registry and package entry points','partial',
 'Support legacy callers that omit an explicit stream function and allow applications/tests to replace or clear the fallback.',
 'The module owns one mutable defaultStreamFn. setDefaultStreamFn assigns the supplied function or undefined on every call; getDefaultStreamFn throws the exact error below while empty. Agent construction resolves a missing function immediately, including untyped callers that omit options entirely. The raw loop resolves its fallback after emitting initial lifecycle/prompt events. The main index reexports the loop, Agent, proxy, types, harness/session/compaction/resource helpers; the node entrypoint additionally reexports NodeJsExecutionEnv. Importing the agent package alone does not choose a provider. This is process/module state only, with no file or environment-variable configuration.',
 quote('stream',1,21)+'\n'+none,
 S('stream',1,21)+'; '+S('agent',225,245)+'; '+S('loop',95,143)+'; ice:packages/agent/src/index.ts:1-81; ice:packages/agent/src/node.ts:1-3. Exports setDefaultStreamFn/getDefaultStreamFn and package barrels. Tests: '+test('agent.test.ts','legacy caller omits streamFn')+'; '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','legacy caller omits streamFn')+'.',
 R('ice-agent/src/stream_fn.rs',44,77)+'; '+ar(250,305)+'. Rust uses OnceLock: first Some wins, later replacements and None are ignored. Missing fallback errors differ in wording and Result boundary. No Rust equivalent is needed for Node-specific import paths, but default replacement/clear semantics are a real SDK gap.',[2],'P2',[30,65]);
add(spec,4,'Raw loop entry, continuation, sink and return boundaries','different-by-design',
 'Start or continue a run with explicit lifecycle events, preserving which messages are considered newly produced.',
 'runAgentLoop clones the context object and transcript array, initializes newMessages with the supplied prompts, emits agent_start then turn_start, emits start/end for each prompt, and enters the loop. Prompt objects themselves are not deep-cloned. runAgentLoopContinue rejects an empty transcript or an assistant tail, permits any other custom last role, emits only agent_start/turn_start, and initializes newMessages empty. Its shallow context copy retains the original messages array, so raw continuation appends can mutate the caller array; Agent avoids that by supplying its own snapshot.\n\nThe sink may return void or a promise and every explicit await is an ordering/backpressure point. Raw run functions return newly added messages; no session file or event journal is written. agentLoop/agentLoopContinue adapt the sink to EventStream, complete its result when agent_end arrives and end it after the run promise resolves. The adapter attaches only a fulfillment handler; an exceptional raw run can reject without ending the adapter stream. This is an existing failure boundary, not proof of a reliable retry/recovery mechanism.',
 quote('loop',19,93)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',19,143)+'. Exports agentLoop/agentLoopContinue/runAgentLoop/runAgentLoopContinue/AgentEventSink. Tests: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','context has no messages')+'; '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','without emitting user message events')+'; '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','caller responsibility')+'.',
 lr(1,211)+'; '+R('ice-agent/src/events.rs',113,201)+'. Rust exposes async Result-returning run functions with owned context, emitter objects and a LoopOutcome rather than the same EventStream/new-message return API. It also has run_agent_loop_from_assistant for recovery; that is not the TypeScript raw continuation contract. Preserve explicit entry/exit semantics in a compatibility facade rather than depending on ownership aliasing.',[1,2,3],'P1',[120,220]);
add(spec,5,'Turn progression and exact steering/follow-up drain order','partial',
 'Run tools and additional model turns until completion while inserting queued user work only at defined boundaries.',
 'After initial events, poll steering once. The first turn does not emit a second turn_start; subsequent turns do. Inject pending messages in order, emitting start/end before appending each to currentContext/newMessages. Stream one assistant, append it to newMessages, and immediately end on stopReason error/aborted without tools, prepare/stop hooks or queue drains. Otherwise execute tool blocks when present regardless of nonerror stop reason, append finalized tool results in source order, emit turn_end, apply prepareNextTurn, then test shouldStopAfterTurn.\n\nOnly then drain steering. A nonempty tool batch normally causes another turn unless every finalized result requests termination; steering still causes a turn even when the batch terminates. When the inner tool/steering loop would stop, poll follow-ups and begin another turn if any. Error/abort terminal responses leave queued messages untouched. The TypeScript loop has no extra signal-only exit between tool completion and the next stream/follow-up, no turn limit, no batch concurrency limit and no queue-size cap. Cancellation is observed by providers/tools and the dispatch checks, not by inventing a new prompt or retry.',
 quote('loop',148,181)+'\n'+quote('loop',224,275)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',148,275)+'. Internal runLoop. Tests: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','queued messages after all tool calls complete')+'; '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','not all tool results terminate')+'.',
 lr(213,432)+'. Rust largely follows the order, but invokes its additional after_tool_results hook before turn_end and exits Aborted immediately after steering drain when the signal is cancelled and no steering is pending (lines334-347,402-411). That can suppress the additional aborted assistant/follow-up path that TypeScript would attempt. Its explicit LoopOutcome is also extra. Treat early cancellation as a conscious behavioral choice, not full event parity.',[4,10,17,18,19],'P1',[100,180]);
add(spec,6,'Context transformation before provider-message conversion','partial',
 'Let trusted host hooks prune or augment application messages before converting them to provider wire messages.',
 'For each request pass the current AgentMessage array and signal to optional transformContext, await the returned array, then await convertToLlm on that result. Conversion sees custom roles; it is required for raw loop configurations and defaults in Agent to filtering only user/assistant/toolResult. The returned transformed array affects the provider request but is not assigned back to durable/current history by this function; a hook can nevertheless mutate shared message objects/arrays in TypeScript. Build provider Context from the current systemPrompt, converted messages and tools. No automatic token pruning, custom-role rendering, sanitization or trust approval occurs here. Hook failure propagates to the enclosing run boundary.',
 quote('loop',281,300)+'\n'+quote('agent',33,37)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',281,300)+'; '+S('types',144,205)+'; '+S('agent',33,37)+'. transformContext/convertToLlm. Tests: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','transformContext before convertToLlm')+'; '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','custom message types via convertToLlm')+'.',
 hr(26,38)+'; '+hr(191,211)+'; '+lr(437,480)+'. Rust has both hooks in the raw loop but passes owned cloned values and infallible future outputs rather than a fallible promise. The public Agent wrapper hardcodes transform_context=None, '+ar(510,541)+'. Keep the wrapper gap separate from raw-loop support; native AgentHarness has its own transform_context path.',[1,4,25],'P1',[55,105]);
add(spec,7,'Per-request dynamic key and full stream-option forwarding','partial',
 'Resolve the current provider credential for every model request and preserve the caller’s provider options.',
 'Call getApiKey with current model.provider on every request, await it, and use its value when truthy; undefined or empty string falls back to config.apiKey. Then invoke the stream callable with {...config,apiKey:resolvedApiKey,signal}. Thus every SimpleStreamOptions field supplied to the raw loop survives, including sampling, token budget, service tier, metadata, headers, transport, response/payload callbacks and retry hints; loop-only properties also remain in the JavaScript object and provider code ignores them. There is no credential caching, refresh lock, independent retry or secret logging at this layer. A changed model from prepareNextTurn changes the next credential lookup. An exception in the resolver is a run failure.',
 quote('types',144,205)+'\nStream options spread and key fallback: '+S('loop',298,309)+'. '+none,
 S('loop',298,309)+'; '+S('types',144,205)+'; '+S('agent',465,503)+'. getApiKey/streamAssistantResponse. Tests: '+test('agent.test.ts','forwards sessionId')+'; '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','prepareNextTurn snapshot')+'.',
 hr(99,129)+'; '+hr(162,188)+'; '+lr(480,499)+'. Rust raw AgentLoopConfig exposes only a curated subset and to_stream_options explicitly sets headers/metadata=None; other provider fields use defaults. An empty Some key is retained instead of falling back. Agent build_config supplies no dynamic resolver. AgentHarness can wrap provider streaming separately, so this finding is the public low-level/Agent API gap, not a blanket claim that CLI headers or OAuth are unavailable.',[2,6,18,25],'P1',[95,160]);
add(spec,8,'Streaming assistant reduction and terminal result selection','partial',
 'Project provider stream events into one assistant message without committing a separate message for each delta.',
 'Await the stream factory. On start, take event.partial as the active message, append it to current context, and emit message_start with a shallow copy. Text/thinking/tool-call start/delta/end events are ignored until a start has been seen; afterward replace the current last message with event.partial and emit message_update, retaining the underlying provider event. On done/error, await stream.result rather than trusting only the event payload. Replace the partial message, or append and synthesize message_start if no start arrived; emit message_end and return.\n\nIf iteration ends without a terminal event, await result and perform the same append/replace/end fallback, but not the abort reclassification described separately. No stream-shape validation prevents multiple starts, missing results or malformed partial arrays; no deadline bounds a result promise. Shallow event copies can share content with later deltas. The agent wrapper reducer, not this helper, owns the public transcript. Provider result text/images/signatures/usage are retained without a second parse.',
 quote('loop',310,378)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',281,378)+'. streamAssistantResponse. Tests: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','should emit events')+'; '+test('e2e.test.ts','preserves thinking content blocks')+'.',
 lr(437,576)+'; '+R('ice-agent/src/events.rs',49,64)+'. Rust has equivalent event categories and terminal-result handling but uses Arc snapshots/owned contexts and a Result error for missing terminal data. It cannot expose arbitrary shared-object mutation semantics. Cancellation normalization and payload-option differences are separate gaps; do not replace the stream with a plain final-string call.',[1,2,4],'P1',[90,160]);
add(spec,9,'Terminal error-to-aborted reclassification','missing',
 'Do not report a provider error as an ordinary failure when the user already cancelled the run.',
 'In the done/error event branch, await response.result; if finalMessage.stopReason is exactly error and the run AbortSignal is already aborted, mutate it to aborted before message_end and turn_end. Keep the provider errorMessage, usage and content unchanged. Do not convert stop/length/toolUse merely because cancellation happened. The EOF-with-result fallback does not apply this normalization, so the contract is narrower than “all errors after cancellation”. This changes downstream retry/notice classification and stops the loop through its normal aborted terminal path.',
 quote('loop',350,359)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',350,378)+'. streamAssistantResponse. Test: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','reclassifies an error response as aborted')+'.',
 lr(515,576)+'; '+R('ice-agent/src/abort.rs',1,94)+'. The Rust stream reduction preserves the returned error stop reason. Abort helpers provide cancellation primitives but not this normalization. The separate post-tool LoopOutcome::Aborted exit is not equivalent to correcting the assistant message.',[8],'P1',[12,30],{reason:'Cancellation must not be mistaken for a retryable provider failure or leave misleading persisted stop reasons.'});
add(spec,10,'Whole-batch sequential override and truncated-call rejection','partial',
 'Protect truncated tool arguments and respect tools that require serial execution.',
 'Collect every assistant content block whose type is toolCall in source order. If stopReason=length, never execute any of those calls, even if some arguments look complete. Emit start, an error execution-end, then tool-result message start/end for each, using the exact error below, and return terminate=false so the model can reissue. Missing/blocked tools are not consulted on this path.\n\nFor other nonterminal responses choose sequential when config.toolExecution=sequential OR any named tool in the current registry has executionMode=sequential; otherwise parallel. Unknown tool names do not force sequential. In sequential mode emit start, prepare, execute/finalize or use the immediate error, emit execution-end and message start/end before the next tool. After each finalized call, stop dispatching remaining calls if the signal is aborted; do not synthesize results for never-dispatched remainder. CurrentContext is not appended with batch results until the entire batch returns, although event consumers see sequential result messages immediately. No per-batch size or concurrency cap exists here.',
 quote('loop',388,433)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',388,493)+'; '+S('types',34,51)+'. failToolCallsFromTruncatedMessage/executeToolCalls/executeToolCallsSequential. Tests: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','length-truncated')+'; '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','one of multiple tools has executionMode')+'; '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','all tools have executionMode=parallel')+'.',
 lr(581,688)+'. Rust has the same batch-mode and length rejection structure; error details serialize as null instead of the TypeScript empty object, and common cancellation/update/finalization differences still apply. This entry is partial, not a missing scheduler. Sequential execution remains a whole-batch property, not a per-tool mutex.',[12,13,14,15,16,17],'P1',[45,90]);
add(spec,11,'Parallel completion events during later permission preparation','partial',
 'Show a finished tool immediately even while a later tool is waiting for validation or user approval.',
 'Prepare calls serially in assistant order. Emit tool_execution_start before each preparation. An immediate missing/blocked/aborted/invalid outcome emits execution-end immediately and becomes an already-resolved slot. A prepared call starts execute and finalize at once in an async closure; that closure awaits its execution-end sink before resolving. Later beforeToolCall work therefore does not delay earlier tool updates or end events. Attach a catch to running promises only to prevent temporary unhandled rejection while preparing later calls; Promise.all still observes failure.\n\nAfter all dispatched calls settle, iterate Promise.all results in source order and emit tool-result message start/end. Completion events follow actual finalization/sink progress, transcript results follow source order. Cancellation stops starting further preparation after the current slot but does not abandon already-running promises; they receive the same signal and are awaited. There is no independent worker count, timeout or cross-tool isolation.',
 quote('loop',496,565)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',496,565)+'. executeToolCallsParallel. Tests: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','completion order but persist tool results in source order')+'; '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','later call still awaits beforeToolCall')+'.',
 lr(700,877)+'. Rust spawns execute/finalize while preparing later calls, but the task does not emit execution-end. End events are collected only after the preparation loop finishes (lines745-756 and758 onward). If several handles finished during that wait, the is_finished scan emits them in source-index order, not their original completion order. Panics also lose original call identity through a <panic> placeholder. Preserve identity and emit finalization events inside tasks or a completion channel that is drained during preparation.',[10,12,14,15],'P0',[130,220],{reason:'Approval waits must not hide completed work or break lifecycle ordering relied on by UI, observability and persistence.'});
add(spec,12,'Argument preparation, validation and trusted before-tool hook','partial',
 'Validate model arguments once, then allow a trusted host hook to inspect, block or deliberately rewrite the validated value.',
 'Find the first registry tool whose name exactly equals toolCall.name; missing returns Tool <name> not found with no hook. Run optional synchronous prepareArguments(raw arguments). If it returns the same object retain the original call; otherwise create a call copy with the prepared arguments only for validation. Pass it to validateToolArguments. Then invoke beforeToolCall with original assistantMessage, original raw toolCall, validated args and currentContext, plus signal. A hook can mutate args in place and execution receives that mutation without revalidation. The raw toolCall stays distinct unless shared references were mutated.\n\nAfter awaiting the hook, cancellation wins over a block decision. A truthy block emits an error using reason when nonempty/truthy, else Tool execution was blocked. There is no permission prompt in the loop itself and a missing hook means no additional policy gate. Preparation/validation/hook exceptions become immediate error results; afterToolCall is not invoked for these immediate paths. The AI validator’s schema/coercion contract is a separate dependency, not implied by a substring match.',
 quote('loop',595,673)+'\n'+quote('types',61,64)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',595,673)+'; '+S('types',93,119)+'. prepareToolCallArguments/prepareToolCall. Tests: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','mutated beforeToolCall args without revalidation')+'; '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','prepare tool arguments for validation')+'.',
 lr(922,1023)+'; '+tr(105,152)+'; '+R('ice-agent/src/agent_tool.rs',43,47)+'. Rust represents mutation by returned args, which correctly bypasses revalidation, but supplies the prepared call rather than original raw call to the before hook. Some("") block reason is retained rather than using the fallback; before hooks also have a Rust-only terminate result. Hooks return infallible Option futures, so TypeScript exception-to-result behavior has no direct Result channel. Keep trusted overrides separate from untrusted model input.',[1,10,16],'P0',[90,160],{reason:'Changing the raw-versus-validated boundary can invalidate permission matching and audit evidence.'});
add(spec,13,'Final cancellation check immediately before tool execution','partial',
 'Close the asynchronous gap between successful preparation and dispatch of a side-effecting tool.',
 'prepareToolCall checks abort after an awaited before hook and again before returning prepared. executePreparedToolCall checks the same signal once more at its own entry because awaiting preparation yields to the event loop. If cancelled, return Operation aborted/isError=true without calling tool.execute or generating tool updates. It still enters afterToolCall through the executed-outcome path; this is different from cancellation detected during preparation, whose immediate result bypasses afterToolCall. Cancellation after execute begins is cooperative: pass the signal, await the tool, and do not automatically undo or replay it.',
 quote('loop',675,686)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',628,665)+'; '+S('loop',675,719)+'; '+S('loop',722,766)+'. executePreparedToolCall. Tests: abort behavior in '+test('e2e.test.ts','handles abort during streaming')+'; no dedicated test isolates the microtask gap; the guard is explicit source.',
 lr(979,1069)+'. Rust checks cancellation in preparation but execute_prepared_tool_call begins by installing update state and calls execute without an equivalent entry recheck. A cancellation arriving between those stages may still invoke a mutating tool, even though it receives a cancelled token. Require the host-side dispatch check as well as cooperative tool cancellation.',[12,15],'P0',[15,35],{reason:'A cancelled operation must not cross the tool side-effect boundary merely because preparation already succeeded.'});
add(spec,14,'Tool update acknowledgement, late-update suppression and raw arguments','partial',
 'Keep all accepted progress updates ordered before a tool’s final event and ignore callbacks after execute settles.',
 'Each prepared execution creates acceptingUpdates=true and an array of sink promises. The supplied callback ignores calls after the gate closes; while open it invokes the sink immediately with tool_execution_update, original call ID/name/raw arguments and partialResult, wraps its return in Promise.resolve and retains the promise. After execute resolves or throws set the gate false, await all retained update promises, then return the success/error outcome. finally also closes the gate. No rate limit, count cap or payload cap is imposed.\n\nA sink rejection can propagate: the catch path awaits the same failed promises again. A callback that throws synchronously while invoking the sink can become a tool execution error. Concurrent tools have separate gates and can interleave sink calls; this is not a global serialized event queue. Accepting an update means its asynchronous consumers finish before execution-end, not merely enqueueing a broadcast. Raw arguments in update events intentionally differ from prepared/mutated execute params.',
 quote('loop',684,719)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',684,719)+'. executePreparedToolCall update callback. Tests: '+test('agent.test.ts','updates after the tool execution settles')+'; '+test('agent.test.ts','settled parallel tool update')+'.',
 lr(1025,1069)+'; '+R('ice-agent/src/events.rs',113,165)+'. Rust uses an AtomicBool gate and synchronous try_emit with prepared args. It does not await downstream asynchronous update processing; the default try_emit implementation drops updates entirely. Native task-local ToolExecutionScope tracks other host callbacks but is not an acknowledgement for this update stream. Add an owned per-execution update drain/acknowledgement mechanism without holding registry locks across callbacks.',[11,12,13],'P0',[110,200],{reason:'Final events must not overtake accepted updates or expose different arguments to policy/telemetry consumers.'});
add(spec,15,'After-tool override and exception conversion','partial',
 'Let trusted hooks inspect executed outcomes and replace selected result fields before any final event or persisted tool message.',
 'Only a prepared/executed path reaches afterToolCall, including execute exceptions converted to an error and cancellation caught at the execute-entry guard. Supply original assistant/raw call, final prepared args, result, isError and currentContext with signal. If the hook returns a value, spread the original result then replace content, details, usage and terminate using nullish fallback; isError also uses nullish fallback. Empty content arrays and false are meaningful overrides, null/undefined do not erase a field. Preserve addedToolNames and any untyped extra original properties. No deep merge or schema validation follows. A hook exception replaces the entire result with a text error/details={} and sets isError=true, losing prior usage/termination. There is no retry of the tool or hook.',
 quote('types',79,119)+'\n'+quote('loop',722,766)+'\n'+none,
 S('types',79,119)+'; '+S('loop',722,766)+'. finalizeExecutedToolCall. Tests: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','afterToolCall to mark a tool batch as terminating')+'.',
 lr(1071,1119)+'; '+tr(124,152)+'; '+hr(82,91)+'. Rust has field-by-field optional overrides but Some(JSON null) can replace details whereas TypeScript null retains the original. The hook type has no error return and a panic is not converted equivalently; parallel task panic eventually yields a placeholder call, sequential panic can unwind. Provide explicit hook errors and preserve call identity. Do not weaken denial by treating a failed hook as success.',[12,13,14,16],'P0',[75,140],{reason:'Post-tool policy and result sanitation need deterministic failure semantics and stable call identity.'});
add(spec,16,'Tool-result normalization, empty content and additional tool discovery','partial',
 'Persist a model-visible result separately from the richer execution result without letting missing content enter history.',
 'createErrorToolResult produces exactly one text block and details={}, with no usage or terminate property. Execution-end carries the full result plus isError. createToolResultMessage keeps call ID/name, uses result.content??[] so untyped JavaScript tools with missing/null content become an empty array, copies details/usage, includes addedToolNames only when its length is truthy, adds isError and Date.now timestamp. It does not persist terminate or arbitrary other result properties. Emit message_start then message_end for the result. No tool result schema is revalidated here; callers can return malformed nonnull content. Empty/missing content is a compatibility normalization, not a guarantee of safe text.',
 quote('loop',769,805)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',769,805)+'; '+S('types',355,378)+'. createErrorToolResult/createToolResultMessage/emitToolResultMessage. Tests: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','should handle tool calls and results')+'; missing-content fallback is explicit source, with no dedicated assertion in the inspected agent tests.',
 lr(1155,1250)+'; '+tr(37,92)+'. Rust native result content is always a vector and details defaults to JSON null; optional usage/additional names have corresponding fields. The native C-ABI JSON decoder must normalize omitted/null content at its boundary to achieve TypeScript behavior. Current low-level errors use null rather than {}; this is observable in JSON snapshots and hook payloads.',[1,10,15],'P1',[50,95]);
add(spec,17,'All-results termination vote','partial',
 'Stop automatic tool continuation only when every dispatched result explicitly requests termination.',
 'For sequential or parallel finalized calls, terminate is true exactly when the list is nonempty and every final result.terminate===true. No truthy coercion: an untyped 1 or string true does not count. Evaluate after afterToolCall overrides; an error can vote true if a hook explicitly sets it. An immediate not-found/blocked/validation/aborted result has no terminate and prevents the vote. A partial sequential batch cut short by cancellation votes over only finalized calls. The vote suppresses the tool-driven next turn but does not suppress pending steering or follow-up queues. A length-truncated batch explicitly returns false.',
 quote('loop',591,593)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',388,413)+'; '+S('loop',490,493)+'; '+S('loop',561,563)+'; '+S('loop',591,593)+'; '+S('loop',206,267)+'. shouldTerminateToolBatch. Tests: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','every tool result sets terminate=true')+'; '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','not all tool results terminate')+'; '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','afterToolCall to mark')+'.',
 lr(684,687)+'; '+lr(832,835)+'; '+tr(105,122)+'. Rust has the same nonempty/all-results vote for executed results, but before_tool_call can attach terminate to a blocked immediate result, an authority not present in TypeScript. Its post-tool cancellation exit can bypass queue continuation independently of the vote. Native bool types avoid untyped truthiness differences.',[5,10,15,16],'P1',[30,60]);
add(spec,18,'Atomic next-turn context, model and thinking replacement','partial',
 'Apply a host-provided runtime snapshot before the next tool-following or queued-message turn.',
 'After turn_end call prepareNextTurn with current assistant, ordered tool results, current context and all newMessages. Await a result. Replace currentContext if result.context is supplied. If model or thinkingLevel is not undefined, copy config and replace model with result.model??old; preserve reasoning when thinkingLevel is undefined, clear reasoning when off, otherwise use the selected level. Do not change the already-selected stream function. The following shouldStopAfterTurn sees the updated context, and any next request uses the new model/provider and dynamic key lookup. No persistent model-change entry or Agent public-state mutation is automatically written by this raw hook. A returned invalid model/context is trusted host data and is not validated here. Failure propagates, with no fallback snapshot.',
 quote('loop',227,245)+'\n'+quote('types',121,142)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',224,246)+'; '+S('types',121,142)+'. prepareNextTurn/AgentLoopTurnUpdate. Test: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','prepareNextTurn snapshot')+'.',
 tr(197,204)+'; '+lr(360,375)+'. Rust declares all three fields but applies only context. Neither the raw loop nor Agent implements the comment’s claimed ownership of the ignored model/thinking values; Agent does not expose the hook at all. Native AgentHarness config sets prepare_next_turn=None. Implement immutable-per-turn config replacement after the hook, preserving current callback/stream handles.',[5,7,19],'P0',[65,125],{reason:'A requested mode/model transition must actually affect the next provider request and its credential route.'});
add(spec,19,'Stop-after-turn gate before queue consumption','partial',
 'Allow the host to pause after a settled turn without losing messages queued for a later run.',
 'Invoke shouldStopAfterTurn after preparation and before steering/follow-up drains. Pass assistant, ordered tool results, the possibly replaced current context and newMessages. Await boolean; when true emit agent_end(newMessages) and return. No extra assistant text or turn_end is synthesized. Queues remain untouched, including messages queued during a long tool. Error/aborted assistant responses exit earlier and never invoke this hook. The Agent wrapper captures the shouldStopAfterTurn function when building its run config and supplies its active signal; changing that field during the run does not replace the captured function.',
 quote('loop',247,261)+'\n'+quote('agent',465,482)+'\n'+none,
 S('loop',247,261)+'; '+S('agent',465,482)+'. shouldStopAfterTurn. Tests: '+test('agent-loop.test.ts','shouldStopAfterTurn returns true')+'; '+test('agent.test.ts','forwards shouldStopAfterTurn')+'.',
 hr(43,46)+'; '+lr(377,394)+'; '+ar(510,541)+'. The raw Rust gate and order exist, but the public Agent wrapper does not expose/forward it. Native AgentHarness has other stop controls; those do not establish this SDK callback contract. Preserve pending queues rather than draining them before returning.',[5,18,22,25],'P1',[35,75]);
add(spec,20,'Mutable Agent state, snapshots and reducer visibility','different-by-design',
 'Expose current transcript/model/tools and runtime status, with mutations visible before event listeners run.',
 'Initialize systemPrompt="", model=the exact unknown placeholder, thinkingLevel=off, tools/messages=[], streaming=false, no streaming/error message and an empty pending-tool Set, overridden by supplied initial configuration. Copy tools/messages on assignment and initialization but expose the internal arrays on reads; elements and models are not deep-cloned. Public state assignment therefore changes future snapshots and direct array mutation is possible.\n\nReducer sets streamingMessage for any message_start/update, clears and appends on message_end, replaces the pending Set with a copied add/delete set at tool start/end, and sets errorMessage only for assistant turn_end with a truthy error string. agent_end clears streamingMessage but does not mark idle. Reducer changes occur before subscribers. Run start clears prior error and sets streaming; final lifecycle cleanup clears pending state. No filesystem/session persistence is provided by Agent itself.',
 quote('agent',39,95)+'\n'+quote('types',327,353)+'\n'+none,
 S('agent',39,95)+'; '+S('agent',249,289)+'; '+S('agent',564,610)+'. Agent.state/createMutableAgentState/processEvents. Tests: '+test('agent.test.ts','default state')+'; '+test('agent.test.ts','custom initial state')+'; '+test('agent.test.ts','state with mutators')+'; '+test('e2e.test.ts','executes tools and tracks pending')+'.',
 tr(206,271)+'; '+ar(47,153)+'; '+ar(324,328)+'. Rust returns an owned snapshot, supports only an assistant streaming payload and does not expose TypeScript-style state mutation/setter methods. Its placeholder uses Model::new defaults rather than explicitly zeroing every model field here. Preserve Rust ownership, but expose explicit configuration/transcript mutation APIs needed by callers; do not pretend mutating a returned snapshot changes the live agent.',[1,21,25],'P1',[95,175]);
add(spec,21,'Awaited Agent subscribers and the true idle boundary','partial',
 'Ensure a run does not become idle until all registered lifecycle handlers have completed.',
 'subscribe stores callbacks in a Set and returns a closure that removes that exact callback; there is no replay. processEvents reduces state first, requires an active AbortController, then iterates subscribers in Set insertion order and awaits each with the same event and active signal. Handlers can enqueue work or await persistence before the producer advances. A thrown/rejected subscriber enters the run failure path; a failure while emitting that synthetic lifecycle can still reject prompt, but finally cleans runtime state.\n\nagent_end means no further normal loop events, not idle. isStreaming remains true and waitForIdle remains pending while its subscribers run. finishRun clears state and resolves the stored promise only afterward. No listener timeout, maximum count, payload copy isolation or concurrent fan-out is added. Tool-update sink calls from parallel tools can overlap; the rule is per invocation, not a global event mutex.',
 quote('agent',557,610)+'\n'+none,
 S('agent',249,262)+'; '+S('agent',506,562)+'; '+S('agent',604,610)+'. subscribe/processEvents/finishRun. Tests: '+test('agent.test.ts','await async subscribers before prompt resolves')+'; '+test('agent.test.ts','waitForIdle should wait for async subscribers')+'; '+test('agent.test.ts','active abort signal to subscribers')+'.',
 ar(128,153)+'; '+ar(285,322)+'; '+R('ice-agent/src/events.rs',134,165)+'. Rust Agent subscribers receive tokio broadcast messages (capacity256) and are not awaited; slow receivers can lag/drop, no active signal accompanies each callback and completion does not acknowledge downstream persistence. The raw emitter can await a custom sink, but the public wrapper does not expose an awaited subscription path. Add a host-critical awaited sink separate from optional lossy UI broadcast.',[4,14,20,24,27],'P0',[130,230],{reason:'Persistence and shutdown must not treat queued broadcast delivery as completed event handling.'});
add(spec,22,'Steering and follow-up queue CRUD and delivery modes','partial',
 'Queue, inspect, remove and clear pending messages without accidentally changing delivery order.',
 'Use two independent FIFO arrays. Default mode is one-at-a-time; all returns a shallow copy of the entire queue and clears it, while one-at-a-time removes only the first element. enqueue retains the supplied message reference. remove selects the first object-identical message using indexOf, not equal JSON; return false if absent. clear removes every queued item. Agent exposes steer, followUp, removeSteeringMessage, removeFollowUpMessage, clearSteeringQueue, clearFollowUpQueue, clearAllQueues, hasQueuedMessages (either queue), hasQueuedSteeringMessages and mutable steering/follow-up mode accessors. No IDs, persistence, caps, priority promotion or duplicate suppression exist at this layer. Queue mutation is synchronous between JavaScript awaits.',
 quote('agent',125,168)+'\n'+quote('agent',291,345)+'\n'+none,
 S('agent',125,168)+'; '+S('agent',291,345)+'. PendingMessageQueue and Agent queue methods. Tests: '+test('agent.test.ts','reports queued steering separately')+'; '+test('agent.test.ts','only the selected queued steering')+'.',
 R('ice-agent/src/queue.rs',1,120)+'; '+ar(330,355)+'; '+ar(432,443)+'. Rust MessageQueue implements FIFO drains, but public Agent exposes only enqueue and combined has_queued_messages. Selected removal, individual clear/inspection and delivery-mode updates are absent there. reset calls try_drain once per queue, so default one-at-a-time leaves all but the oldest message queued. Native AgentHarness has durable queue APIs; their IDs/semantics differ from this wrapper and should be adapted explicitly.',[5,23,27],'P1',[105,185]);
add(spec,23,'Continuation from an assistant tail using queued work','partial',
 'Resume an otherwise completed agent when steering or follow-up work is waiting, without consuming two one-at-a-time messages at once.',
 'Reject an active run first and reject an empty transcript. If the last message is assistant, drain steering according to its mode. When nonempty, start a new prompt run from those messages and skip exactly the first steering poll inside the loop; later turns poll normally. Otherwise drain follow-ups and run those as prompt messages without the skip. If both drains are empty, throw Cannot continue from message role: assistant. For a nonassistant tail use raw continuation with a context snapshot and no re-emission of its existing last message. Queue entries removed for the new run become the new prompt batch and are not restored on later provider failure.',
 quote('agent',380,408)+'\n'+quote('agent',495,503)+'\n'+none,
 S('agent',380,408)+'; '+S('agent',429,455)+'; '+S('agent',495,503)+'. Agent.continue/runPromptMessages/createLoopConfig. Tests: '+test('agent.test.ts','queued follow-up messages after an assistant turn')+'; '+test('agent.test.ts','one-at-a-time steering semantics from assistant tail')+'.',
 ar(557,577)+'. Rust Agent::continue_run rejects every assistant tail before consulting either queue, despite its comment promising the exception. Its additional run_agent_loop_from_assistant recovery function executes pending tools and is not a substitute for queue-driven new prompts. Implement the one-poll skip so the one-at-a-time contract is preserved.',[4,5,22,26],'P1',[65,115]);
add(spec,24,'Synthetic failure lifecycle and guaranteed Agent finalization','partial',
 'Turn an unexpected thrown run failure into an assistant error message and balanced terminal events.',
 'runWithLifecycle rejects reentrancy, creates one AbortController plus a completion promise, sets activeRun and streaming state before invoking the executor. Catch any executor rejection, create an assistant with one empty text block, current public model identity, shared all-zero usage, stopReason aborted when signal is cancelled else error, error.message or String(error), and current timestamp. Emit message_start, message_end, turn_end with empty toolResults, then agent_end containing only that failure message. Do not replay a tool or re-emit earlier messages.\n\nFinally always clear streamingMessage/isStreaming/pending tool IDs, resolve the idle promise and clear activeRun. If a subscriber rejects while handling the synthetic failure, prompt can still reject, but cleanup runs. Ordinary provider error events already produce a terminal assistant and do not enter this synthetic path. The low-level EventStream adapters lack this wrapper guarantee.',
 quote('agent',506,555)+'\n'+none,
 S('agent',506,555)+'. runWithLifecycle/handleRunFailure/finishRun. Test: '+test('agent.test.ts','full lifecycle events for thrown run failures')+'.',
 ar(391,416)+'; '+ar(449,480)+'; '+ar(544,577)+'. Rust normal returned errors call finish_run and propagate Result, but do not synthesize the TypeScript assistant/event sequence. There is no RAII run guard in this wrapper; dropping/cancelling its future or unwinding a panic can leave active_run and streaming state set. The CLI AgentHarness has separate guards and must be evaluated independently. Add cancellation-safe finalization and an explicit compatibility error-event policy.',[4,8,20,21,27],'P0',[105,185],{reason:'A run failure must not strand the agent as busy or omit the persisted terminal failure that consumers expect.'});
add(spec,25,'Agent option and mutable hook forwarding','partial',
 'Make the raw-loop extension points available through the stateful Agent SDK.',
 'Construction and public fields accept convertToLlm, transformContext, streamFunction, getApiKey, onPayload/onResponse, beforeToolCall/afterToolCall, shouldStopAfterTurn, legacy prepareNextTurn(signal), context-aware prepareNextTurnWithContext(context,signal), sessionId, thinkingBudgets, transport(default auto), maxRetryDelayMs and toolExecution(default parallel). When building a run config snapshot model/thinking/system/tools/messages and most callback references. Context-aware preparation wins over legacy when both are present; its wrapper checks the current preparation fields on each invocation. shouldStopAfterTurn is captured once. The active signal is passed explicitly to legacy/context-aware preparation and stop callbacks.\n\nThinking off becomes undefined reasoning, not a literal provider off. The wrapper intentionally exposes only selected SimpleStreamOptions; the raw loop accepts the larger set. Mutating a provider/callback field mid-run does not uniformly replace a captured reference, so preserve the exact snapshot/dynamic distinction rather than claiming all settings are live. No plugin loading, permission policy or additional loop is introduced here.',
 quote('agent',97,123)+'\n'+quote('agent',465,503)+'\n'+none,
 S('agent',97,123)+'; '+S('agent',188,245)+'; '+S('agent',457,503)+'. AgentOptions/createLoopConfig. Tests: '+test('agent.test.ts','legacy prepareNextTurn signal callback')+'; '+test('agent.test.ts','forwards shouldStopAfterTurn')+'; '+test('agent.test.ts','forwards sessionId')+'.',
 ar(20,46)+'; '+ar(162,172)+'; '+ar(510,541)+'. Rust Agent options/builder carry a much smaller set and hardcode transform, key resolution, before/after/prepare/stop hooks to None. Transport, thinking budgets, payload/response callbacks and retry-delay forwarding are also not exposed equivalently. Raw Rust hooks and the CLI harness can implement parts, but those do not fill the public Agent wrapper surface automatically.',[2,6,7,18,19,20],'P1',[150,260]);
add(spec,26,'Prompt overloads and content-shape preservation','partial',
 'Accept text with optional images, a single typed message or an ordered message batch.',
 'prompt checks busy status before normalization. An array is used directly, a nonstring message becomes a one-element array, and a string always becomes a user message with content=[{type:text,text:input},...images] and Date.now timestamp. Empty strings still create a text block; an empty image list adds nothing. Images supplied alongside a message/array are ignored because those overload paths return early. No input trim, image validation, size limit, path loading or policy prompt occurs in Agent. Initial prompt events and newMessages preserve this block-array shape even when the provider later supports plain strings.',
 quote('agent',370,377)+'\n'+quote('agent',410,427)+'\n'+none,
 S('agent',370,377)+'; '+S('agent',410,441)+'. prompt/normalizePromptInput. Tests: '+test('e2e.test.ts','handles a basic text prompt')+'; content-array/image behavior is specified directly in normalizePromptInput.',
 ar(376,403)+'. Rust has text/single/batch convenience methods, but text constructs UserContent::Text and there is no text-plus-images convenience overload. A caller can construct UserMessage blocks manually, so image capability is not wholly missing. A TypeScript-compatible adapter should normalize the public event/persistence shape rather than merely relying on provider equivalence.',[1,4,20],'P2',[25,55]);
add(spec,27,'Abort, wait-for-idle and reset lifetime rules','partial',
 'Provide cooperative cancellation and a stable completion wait while allowing explicit state reset.',
 'Agent.abort aborts only the current controller and is a no-op while idle. signal getter is active signal or undefined. waitForIdle awaits the current run promise, or an already-resolved promise if none; it includes awaited subscribers. reset synchronously clears transcript, streaming/error/pending state and both entire queues, but keeps model/tools/system/options and does not abort or reject an active run. Calling reset during execution can therefore be followed by fresh events that repopulate state; do not describe it as a cancellation primitive. Only runWithLifecycle owns activeRun cleanup. There is no timeout on waiting and no recursive child/process cancellation in the Agent wrapper.',
 quote('agent',347,368)+'\n'+none,
 S('agent',347,368)+'; '+S('agent',506,555)+'. abort/signal/waitForIdle/reset. Tests: '+test('agent.test.ts','abort controller')+'; '+test('agent.test.ts','waitForIdle should wait')+'; '+test('e2e.test.ts','handles abort during streaming')+'.',
 ar(358,480)+'. Rust abort is equivalent cooperative token cancellation, but wait_for_idle clones a Notify then registers its wait after releasing the lock; notify_waiters can occur in between, producing a missed wakeup. This is a source-derived race, not runtime-tested. reset rejects active runs, leaves queued tail items via one try_drain, and takes state then active_run locks opposite to start_active_run (active_run then state), allowing a concurrent deadlock. Preserve Rust’s safer reject-active policy deliberately, fix queue clearing/lock order, and use a retained completion future/watch state.',[20,21,22,24],'P0',[95,175],{reason:'Completion waits, reset and cancellation must not hang or retain stale queued work.'});
